{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"A whole-fund conversion generally turns existing mutual-fund shares into ETF shares; it doesn’t leave investors choosing between share classes.\n\nCheck the fund’s official notices and prospectus supplement for the effective date, any election or account-update deadlines, and what happens if you do nothing. Procedures vary. Direct holders may need to open or update an ETF-capable brokerage account, provide tax or identity information, or make transfer or registration changes. Confirm whether the fund or transfer agent will move shares automatically or needs your instructions.\n\nAfter conversion, ETF shares trade through a brokerage during market hours, and direct mutual-fund purchase or redemption services may end. Check how fractional shares will be handled—they may be paid out in cash, sold, or treated another way. Don’t assume deadlines, trading availability, or fractional-share treatment; verify them in the fund’s notice."}}
